## Summary

This PR consolidates #37428, #37429, and #37433 into one reviewable implementation of #37405.

The combined diff changes **199 files**, removes **11,813 lines**, adds **1,186 lines**, and is **net -10,627 lines**. It combines evidence-based test cleanup with backend deduplication, incremental CI admission rules, a first kind-first taxonomy tranche, and a shared CUDA diffusion CI entry point.

The audit identifies candidates; it is not an automatic deletion oracle. Tests covering numerical/reference parity, model loading, quantization, scheduler/cache state, distributed behavior, APIs, or hardware-specific execution were retained unless an equal or stronger active test covered the same regression.

## Consolidated scope

`test/manual/` is intentionally left untouched: its retirement is deferred to a separate discussion (the tree still receives new files).

### CPU-lane double-registration tax (new)

18 mlx test files were registered into both `stage-a-unit-test-mlx` (the macOS runner that has mlx) and `base-a-test-cpu` (Linux, where no dependency extra installs mlx). On the Linux lane every one of their tests is `_HAS_MLX`-gated, so the CPU registration bought zero assertions while paying the per-file interpreter+import floor (~7s x 18 files on the lane that serially gates every GPU stage) and polluting the partition model with dead `est_time`. The CPU registrations are removed; the mlx-lane registrations stay, and the three mlx files with genuinely CPU-runnable cases keep their CPU registration.

### Test cleanup

- remove permanently disabled registrations, exact/AST-equivalent duplicates, shadowed classes, source-shape ratchets, static export/registry mirrors, and weak-oracle mock tests;
- remove call-mirror unit tests whose assertions only restate the implementation's internal call choreography (constructor kwarg forwarding echoes, log-format echoes, dummy-buffer allocation call echoes, warmup/capture/step call counts) and a meta test for an agent benchmarking script under `.claude/skills/` that is not shipped runtime code;
- trim low-signal assertions from surviving LLM, gateway, and diffusion suites while retaining behavioral boundaries;
- repair and re-enable the tensor-dump forward-hook test instead of deleting it;

### Backend coverage and CI cost

- merge the duplicated Ascend OpenAI function-calling implementation into the shared test while preserving all 12 active NPU methods and NPU-specific model/server arguments;
- remove backend registrations from API/control-plane tests that do not exercise backend behavior;
- remove the disabled aggregate Grok accuracy suite already replaced by active model-specific suites;
- move the expensive radix-cache bit-exact and Eagle stress tests from the default lane to `extra-a`.

### Taxonomy and diffusion dispatch

- move 64 unambiguous registered tests with `git mv` into `test/registered/<kind>/<subsystem>/`:
  - 40 model E2E files -> `e2e/models`;
  - 16 large-model files -> `e2e/models_large`;
  - 2 eval files -> `accuracy/models`;
  - 2 scheduled performance files -> `perf/models`;
  - 4 stress files -> `stress/models`;
- add an incremental taxonomy ratchet for newly added/copied/renamed destinations;
- register seven CUDA diffusion suites and route `pr-test-multimodal-gen.yml` through the shared `test/run_suite.py` discovery/dispatch entry point;
- preserve diffusion's case-aware pytest collection, LPT partition plans, filters, continue-on-error behavior, artifact paths, and historical working directory behind a compatibility bridge;
- keep the package-local diffusion runner as a compatibility shim for non-CUDA callers. This PR does not physically move the entire diffusion test tree.

## Deletion criteria and retained boundaries

Every removal answers the admission-rule question "what future diff would turn this test red?" — when the only answer was "editing the test itself", the test went. Mock usage alone was never a deletion reason. A test was removed only when it could not gate a maintained regression, duplicated stronger active coverage, asserted implementation/source shape or call choreography instead of behavior, was explicitly archived/disabled without an active CI path, or sat in a tree nothing executes.

A full second screening pass over the audit candidate set (161 files / 66K lines flagged mock-heavy) confirmed most flagged files guard real behavior — dispatch decision tables with documented incident history (DeepSeek-R1 perf, MiniMax-M2.5 accuracy), sparse-attention metadata math, PP leader/follower count synchronization, KV-window rollover semantics, CLI argument contracts, component-residency lifecycle state machines — and those stay. Retained coverage also includes kernel numerical parity, dtype/stride/tail behavior, CUDA Graph capture/replay, `torch.compile`, model checkpoint layout, quantization, LoRA, scheduler/offload policy, distributed state, cache ownership, optional-dependency isolation, API request/response behavior, and the SM90 SubBlock numerical tests that currently expose a real CUTLASS DSL failure on H200.

## Validation

Local/static validation on the final branch:

- `check_registered_tests`, `check_no_registered_tests_in_package`, and `check_no_bare_pytest_main` all pass;
- staged `pre-commit run` hooks pass on the touched files;
- a repo-wide reference scan confirms nothing outside the deleted trees imports or dispatches them (workflows, scripts, docs).

The audit tooling used to screen candidates (the AST-based mock/assertion screener and its CSV output) is documented in RFC #37405 and intentionally not checked in: it is a process artifact of this cleanup, and its snapshot would rot on every test-touching merge. The taxonomy validation for the moved files lives in `check_registered_tests.py` and is covered by this PR.

Earlier H200 validation of the pre-rebase branch (35 focused audit/bridge/partitioning tests, the full diffusion unit lane at 2,397 passed / 107 skipped / 7 known-failure baseline, and shared-runner discovery selecting exactly the seven diffusion bridge files) is unchanged by this tranche: the new commits only delete never-executed trees, call-mirror files, and repair docs links.

The maintainer-review corpus sweep scanned all **32,639** human review threads for every final path and the relevant risk terms. A focused diffusion runner/CI sweep reinforced four review requirements addressed here: do not silently skip files, preserve partition/runtime accounting, make registrations discoverable by the actual runner, and validate behavior through the real entry point rather than only mocks.

